Executing-child extension of Nickolaev's #35 harness
===================================================

Pinned dependencies (preserve their authorship):
  https://github.com/nickolaev/multikernel
  48507f176520b11a655344510b1b865b037e527f
  https://github.com/multikernel/kerf
  38daa5687d6748846ffd003599a3bfba29785cd6

The offline packer extends the earlier pre-spawn adapter's #35 newc/gzip
packaging and Kerf runtime. It does not install anything, build dependencies or
replace #35's general harness. Host prerequisites include Python, Click,
libfdt/_libfdt, BusyBox at /usr/lib/initcpio/busybox, cpio, gzip and QEMU x86.
It reports missing prerequisites instead of installing them. Run it only with
clean dependency checkouts and a clean kernel source at the built revision.

Use x86_child.config requirements in a fresh build directory's .config, then
build with the same-source GCC wrapper. It records the explicit clean source
revision/tree, build command, stable config and both resulting binary hashes in
mk-x86-build.json only after a successful build. Packaging refuses missing,
stale or mismatched proof. Do not overwrite historical builds or proof files.
Example (explicit paths):

  python3 -B build_x86_child.py --source "$source" \
    --kernel-build "$build" --jobs=4

  python3 prepare_x86_child.py --prepare --revision "$revision" \
    --source "$source" --kernel-build "$build" \
    --harness "$nickolaev_harness" --kerf "$kerf" --output "$output_parent"

Without --prepare this is read-only preflight. --prepare makes a fresh bundle
under output_parent, copies the same Python runtime into two root filesystems,
and freezes inputs and SHA256SUMS before launch. The child initrd has no kernel
payload. The supplied output parent must already exist.

Boot ONLY in a disposable VM, never on the machine running these tests:

  timeout --kill-after=5s 240s qemu-system-x86_64 \
    -machine q35 -accel tcg,thread=multi -cpu max -smp 6 -m 3072 \
    -kernel "$bundle/bzImage" -initrd "$bundle/host-initrd.cpio.gz" \
    -append 'console=ttyS0,115200 rdinit=/init panic=0 kernelcore=1G log_buf_len=4M mk_test=x86-host nokaslr' \
    -nographic -monitor none -nic none -no-reboot

Capture the complete command, QEMU version, raw serial output and actual timeout
exit code. Both VM exit and verifier must succeed; do not let tee hide failures.
Run the frozen verifier with an independently pinned source revision and input
bundle; it checks build-bound binaries/config and the frozen manifest too:

  python3 -B "$bundle/verify_x86_child.py" --bundle "$bundle" \
    --revision "$revision" --halt=requested "$serial_log"

Run both test_verify_x86_child.py and test_x86_provenance.py unit suites.
Hash evidence separately after copying it; the original input bundle manifest
does not attest output logs added after launch.

The halt driver drains the child console after stop confirmation and before
unload. A partial last record or child FAIL prevents success. The verifier
requires the nonce-correlated CHILD_CAPTURED boundary and rejects kernel
warnings, BUG/Oops, lockups and RCU-stall diagnostics even with PASS markers.

Live lockdep evidence is mandatory, not inferred from CONFIG_LOCKDEP or taint.
The existing /proc/lockdep_stats must contain exactly one debug_locks field
with value 1. Host BEGIN and HOST_SURVIVED, child CHILD_ALIVE and the
nonce/boot-ID-correlated CHILD_LOCKDEP_FINAL carry fresh reads. The child is
queried before termination; the host is checked after cleanup. Frozen bundle
validation also requires the strict lockdep/RCU/atomic-sleep/preemption options
in x86_child.config. Missing, disabled, malformed, duplicate, stale or
out-of-order samples fail qualification. No skip mode or new kernel API.

These are boundary observations, not continuous monitoring or proof of the
stopped child's final instruction. Kernel diagnostics are still rejected
independently. Once stopped, a child cannot answer another proc query.

Spawn RX session lanes (test-only integration branch)
---------------------------------------------------
Use the existing --profile=tty bundle, eight-CPU x86-tty-host QEMU command,
and mk_tty_mode=spawn-close, spawn-hangup, spawn-overflow or spawn-offline.
Pass the same --mode to its frozen verify_x86_tty.py. These modes require
CONFIG_MK_X86_NATIVE_CONCURRENCY_TEST and the existing boot opt-in/QEMU guard.
Each is a fresh one-shot VM, never run on a production kernel or host.

Close/hangup holds an actual spawn RX invocation after dequeue and before
flip insertion, observes the actual cancellation completion wait, then
releases it. A new TTY session must receive only a fresh nonce-tagged payload.
Hangup records the real driver entry and final ldisc flush, and keeps an old
hung-up file open through the freshness verdict so last-close cleanup cannot
substitute for that flush. The observer requires actual late inserted bytes.

Overflow holds before dequeue, delivers 20000 bytes through the real host TTY,
and requires 16384 FIFO bytes accepted, 3616 dropped, and exact accepted-byte
delivery. The expected dropped-input warning is not a lockdep warning.

Offline uses explicitly injected queue_work_on placement on child logical
CPU2. The same real RX invocation must start there, remain held across
removal into the child's local pool, and continue on another online CPU
before CPU2 is returned through the existing pool overlay. The physical ID
is resolved separately from logical CPU2. Return must restore ownership and
online state before retiring the temporary 16 MiB park-storage grant.
Ordinary host routing targets the child's first CPU; sender affinity alone
would not exercise this condition. This lane covers ordinary CPU hotplug,
via local pool departure/return, not parent-to-child hot-add,
pending-but-never-started work, or every scheduler.
The nested pool prerequisite still runs separately before each session lane.
Direct cpu2/online=0 then =1 failed to re-online the CPU in an earlier run;
that ordinary-hotplug failure remains open and is not fixed by this lane.

TTY_HELD is a snapshot taken before release and printed after stdout is
available again; kernel event ordering supplies the causal evidence, not its
serial-print time. All modes retain full child kmsg transfer, live lockdep,
confirmed halt/unload and host-survival requirements. Observer task references
are retained for this one-shot VM. All observer hooks are excluded from the
production submission; no new production ABI is introduced.
Raw-mode setup uses TCSANOW: the harness must not discard stale input itself.
The drain observer requires workqueue's from_cancel identity, not an ordinary
flush_work barrier. Selected-worker completion plus a trailing readability
check does not independently prove all later RX/flip work quiescent. No such
whole-path qualification is claimed by these lanes.

This boot slice requires six distinct physical IDs resolved from /proc/cpuinfo:
two host CPUs, two assigned child CPUs and two spare pool CPUs. The host gives
the child 512 MiB, loads the real ELF Image plus child initrd, and requires both
assigned CPUs online, PID 1, a distinct boot identity and a nonce-matched reply
over mktty. Requested halt must report all CPUs parked before LOADED; unload
must leave the host alive and instance READY with retained CPU ownership.
The 768 MiB contiguous pool failed intermittently before spawn with KASLR
enabled at 2 and 3 GiB VM RAM. The bounded qualification lane uses 3 GiB plus
host nokaslr; this is not a production allocator fix or KASLR-on qualification.

A PASS is ONLY executing-child boot, requested halt and unload under QEMU TCG.
It is NOT incoming-worker/controller interleaving, IRQ/RCU callback retirement,
failed confirmation, force halt, DMA, crash-kexec or physical-hardware proof.

Additional bounded termination lanes use the same bundle. Add one of
mk_x86_halt=force, mk_x86_halt=poweroff or mk_x86_halt=reboot to the HOST QEMU
command line, and pass the matching --halt option to verify_x86_child.py. The
force lane also requires the native report that NMIs went to both assigned
CPUs before confirmation. Voluntary lanes send a mktty userspace command to
the child and wait for confirmed settlement; they do not call host poweroff or
reboot until all assertions have run. A pass in these lanes does not prove
failed-confirmation retention or callback/concurrency safety.
Healthy nonforce lanes reject NMI settlement; every retention lane requires a
separate recovery NMI after clearing the selector. Failure observation, specific
destruction refusal and allocation snapshots must occur in their intended order.

Both init recipes and kernel instrumentation require the recognized QEMU q35
DMI identity in addition to PID1/architecture and explicit boot guards. This
prevents accidental use on ordinary bare metal, not malicious identity spoofing
or proof that an arbitrary VM is disposable. Use only the documented local lane.

Stop-confirmation failure lane (test branch only): build a separate kernel with
CONFIG_MK_X86_NATIVE_CONFIRM_TEST=y; never add it to production. Append HOST
mk_x86_halt=MODE-retain mk_native_confirm_test=1, where MODE is requested,
force, poweroff or reboot, and verify with --halt=MODE-retain. The option
defaults off; without the explicit host boot
parameter no debugfs interface is created and no confirmation is injected.
The default-off build must have no mk_x86_native_confirm_failure symbol.

After a real child handshake, the selector rejects confirmation for the last
assigned physical CPU. Settlement must fail and leave ACTIVE, CPU ownership,
Image, ring, control/context and pool allocations unchanged. File unload must
return EBUSY. The destruction overlay is accepted as a transaction but must
have status failed, a specific kernel EBUSY refusal and a retained instance.
Voluntary lanes wait for the real incoming worker's failed probe counter, not
just an unchanged ACTIVE status that could be observed before the worker runs.
After clearing the selector, a recovery force-halt must confirm all CPUs and
permit unload. This models inability to prove stop, NOT a real CPU proven to
be executing the Image, and does not qualify IRQ/RCU or bulk cleanup races.

Ordinary child CPU re-online diagnostic: use the existing six-CPU halt profile
with HOST mk_x86_reonline=1. After the nonce handshake the child offlines its
logical CPU1 through sysfs, then attempts to online it again. No pool donation,
held RX work, fault selector or kernel observer is involved. Records bind the
nonce and physical CPU to the before/offline/result topology; success also
requires scheduling the child task on that CPU. A write error or deadline
aborts the host workload before halt/unload; the disposable VM then ends.
This diagnostic is not a pool-return pass or a safe-reclamation qualification.
Keep its failed raw trace and compare an independently pinned baseline. The
normal halt verifier must still fail on a failed re-online diagnostic.
The diagnostic forwards mk_cpu_reonline_probe=1 and reports a disabled child
debug_locks value honestly rather than stopping before the probe. A zero value
or any kernel diagnostic remains a failure under the unchanged halt verifier;
the override is never used in ordinary qualification runs.
